            {
                "entry_id": "cec6fd70-d9b1-3675-a923-ff07aaaba148",
                "word": "coronation",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "055b399f-f296-4ecf-b97f-0db62dbd41a4",
                        "entry_id": "cec6fd70-d9b1-3675-a923-ff07aaaba148",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "coronations"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "6b030c49-00b6-3a7d-b8a6-bcf755027611",
                        "entry_id": "cec6fd70-d9b1-3675-a923-ff07aaaba148",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An act of investing with a crown; a crowning."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "also",
                            "attributive",
                            "literally"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "776584a3-6de6-4032-a01f-9e8affdda539",
                                "sense_id": "6b030c49-00b6-3a7d-b8a6-bcf755027611",
                                "text": "[A]nd if vvee be Spouſes of this Bridegroom [Jesus], vvee cannot but (as vvee are exhorted) rejoyce in that the marriage of the Lambe is come, and the day of our ovvn coronation vvith an incorruptible Crovvn of glory."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "719dabd9-1e7c-3f91-8b37-75c896cf48ac",
                        "entry_id": "cec6fd70-d9b1-3675-a923-ff07aaaba148",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An act of investing with a crown; a crowning.",
                            "An act or the ceremony of formally investing a sovereign or the sovereign's consort with a crown and other insignia of royalty, on or shortly after their accession to the sovereignty."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "also",
                            "attributive",
                            "literally",
                            "specifically"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9540d0f6-b244-4d19-966f-1cec5f156734",
                                "sense_id": "719dabd9-1e7c-3f91-8b37-75c896cf48ac",
                                "text": "King Charles III’s coronation is to be much less elaborate compared to his mother’s."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d09ab7fa-f095-4d45-9f1f-3bfe0de5c49c",
                                "sense_id": "719dabd9-1e7c-3f91-8b37-75c896cf48ac",
                                "text": "Some reaſons of this double Corronation / I haue poſſeſſt you vvith, and thinke them ſtrong."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3ba08d1f-7ea1-4318-ba65-30f34d9f0c0e",
                                "sense_id": "719dabd9-1e7c-3f91-8b37-75c896cf48ac",
                                "text": "[John] Fal[staff]. VVhat diſeaſe haſt thou? / [Peter] Bul[lcalf]. A horſon cold ſir, a cough ſir, vvhich I cought vvith ringing in the Kings affaires vpon his coronation day ſir."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8b2072fe-e3c3-43d1-bf04-cb526c78066a",
                                "sense_id": "719dabd9-1e7c-3f91-8b37-75c896cf48ac",
                                "text": "[T]he Lady Anne, / VVhom the King hath in ſecrecie long married, / This day vvas vievv'd in open, as his Queene, / Going to Chappell; and the voyce is novv / Onely about her Corronation."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8e4862d3-06f1-44d6-8ca5-9fda7fc3eac4",
                                "sense_id": "719dabd9-1e7c-3f91-8b37-75c896cf48ac",
                                "text": "It is well known that at the coronation of kings and queens, even modern ones, a certain curious process of seasoning them for their functions is gone through. […] Certain I am, however, that a king's head is solemnly oiled at his coronation, even as a head of salad. […] But the only thing to be considered here, is this—what kind of oil is used at coronations? Certainly it cannot be olive oil, nor macassar oil, nor castor oil, nor bear's oil, nor train oil, nor cod-liver oil. What then can it possibly be, but sperm oil in its unmanufactured, unpolluted state, the sweetest of all oils? Think of that, ye loyal Britons! we whalemen supply your kings and queens with coronation stuff!"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "53a369d9-a40f-43e7-a0bb-fdb7e6aa146d",
                                "sense_id": "719dabd9-1e7c-3f91-8b37-75c896cf48ac",
                                "text": "[George Friederic] Handel's coronation anthem, God save the King, was played by Dr. Camidge [i.e., John Camidge II] on the organ, with sublime effect. The minister and the music were made for each other."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "44abb9ca-0ab9-49fe-a1b5-e5bbc9d5ddf7",
                                "sense_id": "719dabd9-1e7c-3f91-8b37-75c896cf48ac",
                                "text": "Over the mantelpiece, which is high, with brass candlesticks and two ‘Coronation’ tumblers in enamel, hangs a picture of Venice, from one of [William Thomas] Stead’s Christmas Numbers – nevertheless, satisfactory enough."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d9a7e0fb-ac70-400c-8288-64171bd16338",
                                "sense_id": "719dabd9-1e7c-3f91-8b37-75c896cf48ac",
                                "text": "King Charles [III] and Queen Camilla have been crowned on a historic day of pageantry, capped by cheering by crowds in front of Buckingham Palace. Thousands packed the Mall despite the rain, after a deeply religious Coronation service at Westminster Abbey and a huge procession through London. […] The Coronation did not formally change the King's status. Charles became King of the United Kingdom and 14 other realms in September, when his mother Queen Elizabeth II died after 70 years on the throne. […] This time, the ceremony emphasised diversity and inclusion, with more multi-faith elements than any previous coronation, with contributions from Jewish, Muslim, Buddhist and Sikh representatives."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "63570192-bc5c-3ca9-b10e-7cf5069a7e72",
